    "banned_modes": "WFD 2026 WSJT-suite ban carried over verbatim from the fd_rules.rs consts landed in c76f5242 (RTTY and SSTV explicitly legal as Digital).",
    "objectives": "WFD reuses the ARRL bonus menu today (the pre-data modelling); a real WFD objectives table flows through this field later.",
    "exchange": "Class letters are PER EVENT and the two sets are disjoint. arrlfd uses ^[0-9]{1,2}[ABCDEF]$ (ARRL Field Day classes A-F). wfd uses ^[0-9]{1,2}[HIOM]$, from winterfieldday.org/downloads/2026-rules-v3.pdf (the sponsor's own 2026 rules, stamped \"V3 9.8.25\"), read in full 2026-09-07: \"Class Options: H - Home station... I - Indoor station... O - Outdoor station... M - Mobile / Mobile Stationary\", with the worked example \"If you have two stations and you are mobile in East Pennsylvania, you are 2M EPA.\" This CORRECTS a real shipped bug: both events previously used ABCDEF, so no legal WFD class satisfied the required CLASS slot and the RTTY auto-sequencer could not complete a single Winter Field Day contact. The earlier guess of HIOM lived only in a code comment, which is not a source; the sponsor page was unreachable on 2026-08-29 (see the assistance entry) and the PDF is the first-party text that was missing then. Do not change these letters without a verbatim quote + URL + read-date recorded here.",
    "exchange_section_domain": "Both events' SECTION slot uses the derived `fd_sections` domain (the 83 ARRL/RAC sections plus MX and DX). Corroborated for WFD by the same PDF, \"Location Identifier\": \"US and Canadian stations will use the ARRL / RAC Section as designated by those organizations. Mexico stations will use MX, and all other stations outside of the US will use DX.\"",
    "adif_sent_tags": "Every exchange field's adif.sent is \"\" (no standard column that direction; the value rides the private carrier, spec §3.5). MY_CNTY / MY_STATE / MY_ARRL_SECT / STX_STRING / SRX_STRING appear NOWHERE in crates/, src-tauri/ or ui/src (positive control: MY_GRIDSQUARE returns hits, so the grep works and the zeros are real). Batch 6 checks each name against adif.org's own field-list document before any writer emits it; a name that does not check out stays \"\"."
